+    APBR-SAM: Adaptive Perturbation Budget Reallocation for
+                 Post-Training N:M Structured Pruning
+
+                                                                         Abstract
+
+             N:M structured pruning removes weights through discrete support decisions, whereas Sharpness-Aware Mini-
+        mization (SAM) trains with continuous local perturbations. Connecting these stages requires information that persists
+        beyond an individual gradient direction and a selection rule that accounts for interactions between deleted weights.
+        We propose APBR-SAM, which learns positive coordinate scales through perturbation-energy feedback while main-
+        taining a fixed global L2 radius. These scales retain a history of perturbation allocation and express subsequent
+        weight deletions in normalized coordinates. To use this geometry with second-order reconstruction, we introduce
+        a geometry-first conditional selection procedure. Within each 2:4 group, the smallest geometry-normalized weight
+        determines the first deletion. A virtual OBS update then adjusts the remaining weights and inverse curvature, allow-
+        ing the second deletion to reflect the effect of the first. Once both positions are selected, a single joint OBS update
+        compensates them from the current physical weights. This design gives training geometry an explicit role in initiating
+        support selection and uses conditional reconstruction to complete it, connecting historical perturbation allocation to
+        one-shot structured pruning.
+
+I. Introduction
+
+Post-training N:M pruning converts a dense network into a structured sparse model by retaining N weights in every
+group of M . This pattern supports structured sparse execution [1], while imposing a local constraint on which connec-
+tions survive. Dense optimization determines the weights available for pruning; support selection determines which
+of them are removed. We study how information learned during dense optimization can participate directly in this
+later decision.
+
+    Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) provides a natural setting because it explicitly probes parameter changes
+during training [2]. Its perturbation follows the current mini-batch gradient within a prescribed local radius. An
+N:M deletion has a different structure: selected coordinates move to zero, their displacement magnitudes depend
+on the weights, and their locations obey a discrete support constraint. The mismatch concerns direction, scale, and
+support. The training probe explores both future retained and future removed coordinates, whereas the deletion acts
+on a selected support. A persistent description of coordinate exploration can carry information across these two stages.
+
+    APBR-SAM learns this description through adaptive perturbation budget reallocation. Positive coordinate scales
+reweight the first SAM gradient before the perturbation is normalized to a fixed global L2 radius. Global energy
+feedback increases the relative scales of coordinates receiving below-average perturbation energy and decreases those
+receiving above-average energy. Applied recursively, this feedback produces a state that survives individual mini-
+batches. Dense training therefore returns both weights and matched coordinate scales, while retaining SAM’s two-
+gradient optimization procedure.
+
+    Using this state for pruning requires connecting two measurements. APBR expresses the magnitude of deleting
+coordinate i as |wi| exp(−si) in the learned geometry. OBS uses calibration activations to measure the quadratic
+reconstruction cost and the ability of other weights to compensate [3]. The first measurement supplies training history;
+the second supplies activation-dependent interactions. Our design gives each a specific role: geometry initiates a
+support decision, and reconstruction determines how that decision is completed.
+
+    In each 2:4 group, the APBR score selects the first deletion. We virtually apply its OBS update to the remaining
+weights and effective inverse curvature. The second deletion minimizes the resulting conditional OBS cost, so its
+selection reflects the predicted effect of the first removal. The selected pair then receives one joint OBS update
+from the physical state at the group entrance. Conditional updates and joint reconstruction follow established OBS
+principles [3]; APBR provides the training-dependent first choice.
+
+    The resulting pipeline connects historical perturbation allocation to structured reconstruction. Geometry answers
+where deletion begins, conditional curvature identifies its least costly partner, and joint compensation produces the
+sparse weights. This separation also defines a direct experimental comparison: change the first selection signal while
+keeping the dense parent, calibration inputs, conditional update, and final compensation fixed.
+
+                                                                      1
+Our contributions are:
+
+• A persistent coordinate-geometry mechanism that reallocates perturbation energy under a fixed global L2 radius
+   and carries the resulting training state into post-training support selection.
+
+• A geometry-first conditional pruning procedure: APBR selects the initial deletion, the next decision uses its
+   predicted reconstruction effect, and one joint physical OBS update compensates the selected pair.
+
+• A controlled one-shot 2:4 evaluation protocol that isolates the initial geometric decision through matched dense
+   parents, calibration inputs, and reconstruction settings.
+
+II. Related Work
+
+Perturbation geometry during dense training. SAM uses a two-gradient update to optimize loss in a local pa-
+rameter neighborhood [2]. ASAM accounts for parameter rescaling [4], while SSAM restricts perturbations through
+Fisher-guided or dynamically updated masks [5]. GEAR-SAM uses historical gradient energy to allocate a fixed global
+perturbation budget across blocks [6]. APBR-SAM maintains positive coordinate scales through perturbation-energy
+feedback and saves this state with the dense model. The resulting geometry guides a structured deletion decision
+together with activation-based reconstruction.
+
+Preparing dense models for compression. CrAM incorporates a compression operator into optimization to im-
+prove tolerance to subsequent compression [7]. AdaSAP applies importance-dependent perturbations before channel
+removal and sharpness-aware optimization afterward [8]. These approaches connect robust optimization with compres-
+sion. APBR-SAM retains dense two-pass optimization and performs support selection after training. The information
+transferred between stages is a persistent coordinate geometry that expresses deletion relative to the learned scales.
+
+Post-training selection and second-order compensation. Magnitude pruning ranks weight sizes [9], and Wanda
+combines magnitude with input-activation norms [10]. SparseGPT couples support selection with sequential second-
+order compensation [11]; ROSE modifies its processing order using estimated column and block pruning losses [12].
+OBC/ExactOBS develops greedy OBS selection with conditional inverse-curvature updates and joint compensation
+for groups of weights [3]. Our procedure uses these reconstruction tools with two distinct selection signals. Geometry-
+normalized magnitude chooses the first deletion; virtual OBS conditioning supplies the state for choosing its partner.
+The pair receives one joint update over all currently active coordinates.
+
+III. Method
+
+APBR-SAM transfers a persistent training state into a two-stage support decision. The learned geometry selects the
+first deletion in each 2:4 group. A virtual reconstruction step then determines the state in which the second deletion
+is evaluated. Once both positions are selected, joint OBS commits their compensation to the physical weights.
+
