+IV. Theoretical Analysis
+
+A. Training geometry properties
+
+Equation (3) gives ∥ϵt∥2 = ρ whenever its numerator is nonzero. The energy shares also conserve the sum of log-
+scales:
+
+                             si,t+1 = si,t + γ d − d pi,t = 0.                   (13)
+
+i                                         i                    i
+
+Thus det At = i ai,t = 1 in exact arithmetic. This identifies the relative coordinate geometry while the global
+normalization fixes the realized perturbation budget.
+
+    For a fixed gradient with nonzero components, the energy shares are
+
+                             pi = exp(4si + 2 log |gi|) . (14)
+                                       j exp(4sj + 2 log |gj|)
+
+Proposition 1 (Fixed-gradient feedback). For fixed gi̸ = 0, positive γ, and i si = 0, the feedback fixed point has
+pi = 1/d and
+
+                                     s∗i = − 12 log |gi| + 12d log |gj |, a∗i ∝ |gi|−1/2. (15)
+
+                                                                                               j
+
+For d > 1, its linearized eigenvalue on the zero-sum subspace is 1 − 4γ. The strict linear stability range is 0 < γ <
+1/2.
+
+    The fixed-gradient analysis explains how negative feedback balances the coordinate energy shares. Its derivation
+appears in Appendix A.
+
+B. Direct geometry deletion
+
+The geometry score has a separable interpretation. With weights and scales fixed, a direct N:M mask minimizes
+CA(m) by retaining the N largest Ii in each group. Exchanging a retained smaller score with a deleted larger score
+reduces the cost. For the initial decision in Algorithm 1, the same ordering selects the smallest normalized singleton
+displacement.
+
