+VI. Conclusion
+
+APBR-SAM carries information from dense optimization into structured support selection. Global energy feedback
+learns persistent coordinate scales under a fixed perturbation budget. At pruning time, those scales determine the first
+deletion in each quartet; the second decision uses the reconstruction state induced by a virtual OBS update. Joint com-
+pensation then reconstructs the layer for the selected support. This division of roles gives the training geometry a direct
+entry point into pruning while using activation correlations to complete and compensate the decision. The resulting
+method connects historical perturbation allocation, conditional support selection, and second-order reconstruction in
+a single post-training 2:4 pipeline.
+
+A. Feedback Derivation
+
+Squared perturbation coordinates are proportional to a4i gi2, giving Eq. (14). A fixed point with γ > 0 requires pi = 1/d.
+Thus 4si + 2 log |gi| is constant. Enforcing i si = 0 yields Proposition 1.
+
+    Differentiating the shares gives Dsp = 4(diag(p) − pp⊤), so the update Jacobian is
+
+                                          J = I − 4γd(diag(p) − pp⊤).                                   (20)
+
+At p = 1/d, J = I − 4γ(I − 11⊤/d). Every zero-sum direction has eigenvalue 1 − 4γ, giving |1 − 4γ| < 1 precisely
+when 0 < γ < 1/2. The common-scale direction has eigenvalue one and is excluded by the zero-sum constraint. If a
+fixed gradient coordinate is zero while another is nonzero, its share is zero and its log-scale increases by γ each step;
+the component-wise nonzero assumption is required for this interior fixed point.
+
+    For the training perturbation ϵ in Eq. (3), the linearized loss change is
+
+                                          ⊤  g⊤A2g                                                      (21)
+                                          g ϵ = ρ 2 ≤ ρ ∥g∥2 ,
+                                             ∥A g∥2
+
+by Cauchy–Schwarz. Standard SAM maximizes the linearized loss on the sphere; APBR-SAM uses the learned state
+to reweight that direction.
