-\begin{abstract}
-Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM) improves generalization through local parameter perturbations, but its gradient-driven budget allocation is not tailored to the structured deletions imposed by N:M pruning. Standard SAM also retains no persistent coordinate geometry to guide which weights should be removed together. We propose Adaptive Perturbation Budget Reallocation for SAM (APBR-SAM), which learns coordinate scales through global perturbation-energy feedback. The feedback increases the relative scales of coordinates receiving below-average perturbation energy and decreases those receiving above-average energy, encouraging more balanced exploration while preserving SAM's two-gradient training procedure. At pruning time, the learned geometry guides conditional-greedy Optimal Brain Surgeon (OBS) selection: each virtual deletion updates the remaining weights and conditional curvature before the next choice, while geometry-normalized weight magnitudes distinguish candidates with near-minimal second-order cost. The selected set then receives joint OBS compensation from the unmodified current weights. APBR-SAM thereby connects historical perturbation allocation to interaction-aware support selection, without training-time Hessian estimation, exhaustive mask enumeration, or post-pruning fine-tuning.
-\end{abstract}
-
-\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
-Post-training N:M pruning converts a dense model into a structured sparse model by retaining $N$ coordinates in each group of $M$ weights. The local pattern supports structured sparse execution~\cite{nm}, but imposes a discrete decision about which learned weights to remove. Each candidate mask changes the parameter support and produces a specific deletion displacement. The quality of the final model therefore depends on both the dense training trajectory and the support selected after it.
-
-SAM offers a natural starting point for robust dense training~\cite{sam}. It uses the current mini-batch gradient to approximate a worst-case direction in a local Euclidean ball, then updates the model using a second gradient at the perturbed weights. Post-training pruning produces a different kind of parameter change: selected coordinates move all the way to zero, with their locations constrained by the N:M pattern. The displacement is finite, determined by the removed weights, and unrestricted by SAM's training radius. Its direction is set by a discrete mask instead of the current adversarial-gradient construction. Moreover, this deletion direction has nonzero support only on removed weights, whereas the dense SAM perturbation generally spends part of its fixed norm budget on coordinates that the eventual mask retains. Retained-coordinate perturbations still help dense generalization; they are only directionally redundant for representing the structured deletion itself. Local perturbation optimization therefore leaves an explicit scale, direction, and support mismatch between the changes explored during training and those later applied to the model.
-
-A second gap concerns how this limited budget is allocated and what information survives into pruning. Conventional SAM uses a single Euclidean constraint and normalizes the current stochastic gradient, so its finite energy is consumed by the instantaneous direction across both future-removed and future-retained coordinates. The rule maintains no persistent coordinate-scale state describing how that allocation evolves across mini-batches. A subsequent magnitude mask therefore ranks the final weights independently of the exploration history. A shared radius answers how far the local probe can move. It does not align the probe with a support-constrained deletion, nor does it specify how accumulated coordinate geometry should influence which positions are retained within a group.
-
-These gaps motivate a different design question: can the same finite perturbation budget be redistributed across coordinates during training so that its allocation becomes useful for N:M support selection? Changing a common radius only changes the overall exploration scale. Learning coordinate scales instead changes how the fixed budget is spent and turns that allocation into a persistent state that pruning can use. Rather than predicting a mask at every training step, the method learns a geometry that connects local exploration with the later deletion decision, and it keeps SAM's two-gradient update throughout.
-
-APBR-SAM implements this idea with positive coordinate scales initialized to the identity geometry. The scales reweight the first SAM gradient, and global $L_2$ normalization fixes the actual perturbation radius. Each step measures how much of the perturbation energy each coordinate carries. Negative feedback decreases scales with above-average energy share and increases those with below-average share, altering their future directional weights. The state is updated recursively throughout training, so it records the feedback trajectory rather than being assigned from a single final gradient.
-
-After dense training, APBR-SAM retains this state with the model weights and uses their ratio to construct the N:M mask. Deleting a weight moves its coordinate by its full magnitude; dividing that magnitude by the learned scale expresses the deletion in geometry-normalized coordinates. Selecting the largest ratios within each group exactly minimizes the corresponding normalized squared deletion cost. Thus the final score is the support-selection stage of the same geometry-learning process, rather than a detached post-training rule. This direct mask is nevertheless chosen in the APBR diagonal metric, whereas an OBS compensator reconstructs the layer in an activation-induced quadratic metric. We therefore also study a conditional-greedy bridge that preserves the OBS objective and uses APBR geometry only when several physical deletion choices are already near-optimal.
-
-The theory makes this connection precise at two levels. We characterize the feedback dynamics under a fixed nonzero gradient, and we derive the deletion-cost interpretation of the ranking. Under a common transformed-curvature bound along all candidate deletion segments, that cost controls an upper bound on the loss increase. The same groupwise ranking minimizes this common bound with the weights and geometry fixed. Actual post-pruning accuracy remains an empirical question. Our completed CIFAR-100 comparisons evaluate the frozen sparse endpoints against magnitude and activation-aware pruning and second-order compensation baselines.
-
-Our contributions are:
-\begin{itemize}
-\item A persistent coordinate-geometry mechanism that reallocates a finite actual $L_2$ perturbation budget across coordinates using global energy feedback, rather than enlarging the perturbation radius, and preserves the product of coordinate scales in exact arithmetic.
-\item A training-to-pruning transfer through normalized weight scores, with groupwise deletion-cost optimality and a conditional common loss-increment upper bound.
-\item A conditional-greedy pruning rule inside APBR-SAM that re-evaluates later deletions after virtual conditioning, avoids enumerating every N:M support, and applies one physical joint OBS compensation to the selected set.
-\item A structured-pruning evaluation of frozen one-shot endpoints, together with an exploratory comparison of SAM and APBR-SAM under the same conditional selection and compensation rule.
-\end{itemize}
-
-\section{Related Work}
-\paragraph{Sharpness-aware optimization.}
-SAM uses a first gradient to construct a local worst-case perturbation and a second gradient to update the model~\cite{sam}. ASAM makes the neighborhood invariant to parameter rescaling~\cite{asam}, while SSAM sparsifies the perturbation through Fisher-guided or dynamic masks~\cite{ssam}. GEAR-SAM reallocates a fixed perturbation budget across parameter blocks using an exponential moving average of gradient energy~\cite{gear_sam}. APBR-SAM instead learns persistent coordinate-level scales and transfers the resulting geometry from dense training to post-training support selection.
-
-\paragraph{Compression-aware training.}
-CrAM incorporates a target compression operator into optimization and studies one-shot N:M pruning~\cite{cram}. AdaSAP couples adaptive perturbations with channel pruning and fine-tuning~\cite{adasap}. These methods expose the model to compression or pruning during optimization. APBR-SAM instead keeps dense two-pass optimization and constructs the discrete N:M mask only after dense training.
-
-\paragraph{Post-training weight selection and compensation.}
-Magnitude pruning removes small connections and commonly recovers the retained weights by fine-tuning~\cite{han_pruning}. Wanda augments weight magnitude with input-activation norms but performs no pruning-time weight reconstruction~\cite{wanda}. SparseGPT uses calibration activations and an approximate inverse-Hessian procedure to select weights and compensate pruning error in one shot~\cite{sparsegpt}; ROSE reorders SparseGPT's pruning sequence according to estimated column and block losses~\cite{rose}. OBC develops the related ExactOBS framework for post-training pruning and quantization~\cite{obc}. Recomputing saliency after a deletion is therefore an established OBS principle. Our conditional rule specializes it to a local N:M group: the first deletion is virtualized to condition the next score, after which the selected set receives one joint compensation. For N:M sparsity, SR-STE instead trains the sparse model from scratch~\cite{nm}. APBR-SAM differs in that its ranking state is learned during dense SAM training and resolves choices that are nearly tied under the physical OBS objective.
-
-\section{Method}
-\subsection{SAM and a persistent geometric state}
-The training stage learns a relative coordinate geometry without knowing the final mask. We keep SAM's local two-gradient optimization and add a state that persists across the local probes. Let $w_t\in\mathbb R^d$ collect all trainable parameters and $\mathcal L_{B_t}$ be the mini-batch cross-entropy. Standard SAM computes
-\begin{equation}
-g_{1,t}=\nabla_w\mathcal L_{B_t}(w_t),\qquad
-\epsilon_t^{\mathrm{SAM}}=\rho\frac{g_{1,t}}{\norm{g_{1,t}}}.
-\end{equation}
-The second gradient is $g_{2,t}=\nabla_w\mathcal L_{B_t}(w_t+\epsilon_t)$. The model is restored to $w_t$ before the base optimizer applies this gradient. The perturbation is treated as fixed during the second backward pass. Both passes use the same augmented mini-batch.
-
-A single current gradient supplies a probe direction, whereas post-training support selection needs a persistent coordinate comparison. We maintain log-scales $s_t\in\mathbb R^d$, initialized at zero, with
-\begin{equation}
-a_{i,t}=\exp(s_{i,t}),\qquad A_t=\operatorname{diag}(a_{1,t},\ldots,a_{d,t}).
-\end{equation}
-Each $s_{i,t}$ stores the recursively updated relative scale of a parameter coordinate. Its mapping to parameter names, shapes, and coordinates is fixed at initialization, allowing the saved state to be matched to the weights at pruning time. Geometry includes all trainable parameters, including BN affine parameters and biases; the mask later acts on the eligible weight tensors.
-
-\subsection{Redistributing a fixed L2 budget}
-An N:M deletion changes only the coordinates removed by its mask. Standard SAM does not know that mask during dense training and applies a dense probe, so its finite squared-norm budget is distributed across both eventual deletion coordinates and coordinates that remain unchanged by pruning. APBR-SAM does not assume the future support in advance. Instead, it learns a persistent relative allocation from repeated probes and later uses that allocation to decide which coordinates to remove within each group.
-
-To learn an allocation while keeping total exploration fixed, APBR-SAM constructs
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:epsilon}
-\epsilon_t=\rho\frac{A_t^2g_{1,t}}{\norm{A_t^2g_{1,t}}}.
-\end{equation}
-For nonzero numerator, $\norm{\epsilon_t}=\rho$. The denominator fixes the total perturbation energy, while $A_t^2$ changes its coordinate distribution. Holding the gradient fixed, a larger $a_{i,t}$ gives that coordinate more weight before global normalization. A common rescaling of all $a_i$ cancels, so the state represents relative geometry. At initialization, $A_0=I$ gives the usual SAM direction.
-
-This choice separates learning where to explore from changing how far the overall probe moves. The actual perturbations remain on the same Euclidean sphere, while their directions evolve with the learned scales. The second gradient still drives the base SGD update.
-
-\subsection{Global energy feedback}
-The scales are learned from the energy distribution of the current probe. Squared perturbation coordinates measure each coordinate's share of the fixed global energy and remove the sign of the displacement. After constructing the perturbation, we update the log-scales:
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:feedback}
-p_{i,t}=\frac{\epsilon_{i,t}^2}{\sum_j\epsilon_{j,t}^2},\qquad
-s_{i,t+1}=s_{i,t}+\gamma(1-dp_{i,t}).
-\end{equation}
-The reference share is $1/d$. An above-average share decreases the log-scale and therefore its future directional weight; a below-average share increases it. This negative feedback counters concentration in the current energy allocation. Because the update acts on the existing $s_t$, its effect persists across mini-batches. The resulting scales summarize the feedback history of the coordinate geometry. Energy normalization spans the full trainable vector, matching the single global perturbation budget.
-
-The update preserves the sum of log-scales:
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:volume}
-\sum_i s_{i,t+1}=\sum_i s_{i,t}+\gamma\left(d-d\sum_i p_{i,t}\right)=0.
-\end{equation}
-Consequently $\det A_t=\prod_i a_{i,t}=1$ in exact arithmetic. This fixes the common multiplicative scale of the auxiliary geometry while allowing individual coordinates to expand or contract relative to each other. The actual perturbation budget is fixed independently by Eq.~\eqref{eq:epsilon}. Adding a common constant to every $s_i$ leaves its normalized direction unchanged.
-
-A geometry candidate is committed once after a successful SGD update. All-zero perturbations skip feedback. Log-scales use FP64 and model weights use FP32. The log-domain implementation checks finite values and log-volume; numerical settings are given in Appendix~\ref{app:reproduction}.
-
-\subsection{Fixed-gradient characterization}
-For a fixed gradient whose components are nonzero, energy shares take the form
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:softmax}
-p_i=\frac{\exp(4s_i+2\log|g_i|)}{\sum_j\exp(4s_j+2\log|g_j|)}.
-\end{equation}
-This makes the dependence on state explicit and removes the common factor $\rho$.
-\begin{proposition}\label{prop:fixed}
-For fixed $g_i\ne0$, positive $\gamma$, and $\sum_i s_i=0$, the feedback fixed point has $p_i=1/d$ and
-\begin{equation}
-s_i^*=-\tfrac12\log|g_i|+\frac{1}{2d}\sum_j\log|g_j|,\qquad a_i^*\propto|g_i|^{-1/2}.
-\end{equation}
-For $d>1$, its linearized eigenvalue on the zero-sum subspace is $1-4\gamma$. The strict linear stability range is $0<\gamma<1/2$.
-\end{proposition}
-Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed} characterizes the fixed-gradient dynamics; it does not establish convergence under stochastic training. Appendix~\ref{app:proof} gives the derivation and the zero-gradient case.
-
-\subsection{Post-training N:M weight selection}\label{sec:selection}
-Pruning converts the continuous training state into a discrete support choice. A deletion in coordinate $i$ has displacement $-w_i$; in the learned normalized coordinates its magnitude is $|w_i|/a_i$. Using weights and geometry from the same selected dense checkpoint, we compute
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:score}
-I_i=\frac{|w_i|}{a_i}=|w_i|\exp(-s_i).
-\end{equation}
-Each eligible group $G$ of $M$ coordinates retains the $N$ largest scores. The resulting sparse weights are $\widetilde w=m\odot w$, with $\sum_{i\in G}m_i=N$. At equal weight magnitude, a larger learned scale gives a smaller normalized deletion. The ratio therefore uses both the size of the weight removed and the coordinate geometry learned during training. The SAM baseline applies the same grouping constraints to the plain magnitude $|w_i|$.
-
-The relevant parameter change is now the finite deletion displacement, not the training probe. For frozen $A$ and pruning displacement $\delta_m=(m-1)\odot w$, define
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:deletion}
-C_A(m)=\norm{A^{-1}\delta_m}^2=\sum_{i:m_i=0}I_i^2.
-\end{equation}
-Equation~\eqref{eq:deletion} shows what the ratio score optimizes: each deleted coordinate contributes its squared normalized displacement. Retaining the $N$ largest scores exactly minimizes this cost within each group, since exchanging a retained smaller score with a deleted larger score reduces the sum. The final mask thus selects a small deletion in the metric supplied by the learned state.
-
-\paragraph{Connection to pruning loss.}
-To connect that metric to task loss, the analysis follows the entire segment from the dense weights to each candidate pruned model. Let $\mathcal L$ be a fixed loss, with auxiliary model state held fixed, and let $g=\nabla\mathcal L(w)$. A curvature bound shared by these candidate segments makes their normalized deletion costs comparable through a common loss-increment upper bound.
-\begin{proposition}[Conditional pruning-loss bound]\label{prop:pruning-loss}
-Fix $w$, a positive diagonal matrix $A$, and a family $\mathcal M$ of candidate masks. Suppose $\mathcal L$ is twice differentiable along every segment $w+t\delta_m$, and a single $\beta_A\ge0$ satisfies
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:shared-curvature}
-\sup_{\substack{m\in\mathcal M\\ t\in[0,1]}}
-\left\|A\nabla^2\mathcal L(w+t\delta_m)A\right\|_2\le\beta_A,
-\end{equation}
-where the matrix norm is spectral. Then every $m\in\mathcal M$ satisfies
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:pruning-loss-bound}
-\mathcal L(w+\delta_m)-\mathcal L(w)
-\le \norm{Ag}\sqrt{C_A(m)}+\frac{\beta_A}{2}C_A(m).
-\end{equation}
-\end{proposition}
-For $q_m=A^{-1}\delta_m$, the curvature assumption gives the Taylor upper bound $g^\top\delta_m+(\beta_A/2)\|q_m\|_2^2$; Cauchy--Schwarz bounds its linear term by $\|Ag\|_2\|q_m\|_2$. Appendix~\ref{app:pruning-proof} provides the complete argument.
-\begin{corollary}[Minimum common upper bound]\label{cor:mask-bound}
-For fixed $w,A,g$ and the shared $\beta_A$, the function $U_A(c)=\|Ag\|_2\sqrt c+(\beta_A/2)c$ is nondecreasing for $c\ge0$. If $\mathcal M$ consists of independent N:M groups with all other coordinates retained, keeping the $N$ largest $I_i$ in each group minimizes $U_A(C_A(m))$ over $\mathcal M$.
-\end{corollary}
-The resulting chain is explicit: the learned state defines the normalized deletion cost, groupwise ranking minimizes that cost, and the common curvature condition transfers this minimum to the stated upper bound. The comparison fixes $w,A,g$ and $\beta_A$ across masks. Realized loss changes and accuracy are assessed at the frozen post-pruning endpoint. Mask construction uses $I_i$ directly, without estimating $\beta_A$.
-
-Groups follow a fixed stored order, and ties retain lower indices. The concrete structured pattern and implementation details used in the experiments are specified in Appendix~\ref{app:reproduction}.
-
-\subsection{Conditional-greedy OBS refinement}\label{sec:conditional-greedy}
-The direct APBR score in Eq.~\eqref{eq:score} and an OBS compensator measure different objects. The former ranks deletion displacement in the learned diagonal geometry; the latter minimizes a calibration-based quadratic reconstruction objective. Selecting a mask solely in one metric and compensating it in the other can therefore create a selection--compensation mismatch. The conditional-greedy stage of APBR-SAM retains the physical OBS objective as a guardrail and introduces APBR geometry only among physically near-optimal choices.
-
-Consider one output row of an eligible layer. Let $H_\delta=H+\delta I$ be the damped activation Gram matrix and let $Q=H_\delta^{-1}$. During blockwise processing, previously processed columns are frozen. We write $R$ for the resulting conditional inverse curvature over the currently free coordinates and use its principal block for the current N:M group; $w$ denotes the corresponding current weights. For a singleton candidate $i$ in that group, the physical OBS deletion cost is
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:conditional-first}
-c_i=\frac{w_i^2}{2R_{ii}}.
-\end{equation}
-The first deletion is $i^*=\arg\min_i c_i$. Rather than scoring the second deletion in the same pre-deletion state, we virtually condition on removing $i^*$. For all remaining coordinates $j,k\ne i^*$,
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:conditional-update}
-\bar w_j=w_j-\frac{R_{ji^*}}{R_{i^*i^*}}w_{i^*},\qquad
-\bar R_{jk}=R_{jk}-\frac{R_{ji^*}R_{i^*k}}{R_{i^*i^*}}.
-\end{equation}
-The second deletion is selected by
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:conditional-second}
-j^*=\arg\min_{j\ne i^*}\frac{\bar w_j^2}{2\bar R_{jj}}.
-\end{equation}
-Equations~\eqref{eq:conditional-update}--\eqref{eq:conditional-second} make the second decision aware of the first deletion's predicted reconstruction effect. They are used only for support selection. They do not commit a sequential weight update.
-
-After obtaining $D=\{i^*,j^*\}$ for 2:4 sparsity, the method returns to the unmodified current weights and applies one physical joint OBS solution,
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:joint-obs}
-\Delta w=-R_{:,D}(R_{DD})^{-1}w_D,\qquad
-C_{\mathrm{OBS}}(D)=\frac12w_D^\top(R_{DD})^{-1}w_D.
-\end{equation}
-This update sets the selected coordinates exactly to zero and compensates the surviving coordinates jointly. The virtual first deletion therefore affects which set is selected, while Eq.~\eqref{eq:joint-obs} determines the only committed compensation. Repeating the conditioning step $M-N$ times extends the construction beyond 2:4 without enumerating all $\binom{M}{M-N}$ deletion sets; it remains a greedy approximation and is not claimed to recover the globally optimal set.
-
-\paragraph{Near-optimal APBR tie-break.}
-To couple the learned geometry to this physical selection rule, at each stage we first form the near-optimal set
-\begin{equation}\label{eq:near-opt-window}
-\mathcal C_\tau=\left\{k:c_k\le(1+\tau)\min_r c_r\right\}.
-\end{equation}
-The physical arm takes the minimum-cost member. APBR-SAM instead selects within $\mathcal C_\tau$ the smallest normalized deletion magnitude $|w_k|\exp(-s_k)$; for later stages, $w_k$ is replaced by its virtual conditioned value. We fix $\tau=0.01$ before final evaluation. The geometry never changes $H$, damping, $R$, or Eq.~\eqref{eq:joint-obs}; it can only resolve choices whose physical singleton costs lie within one percent of the minimum. Setting $\tau=0$ recovers the physical conditional-greedy path.
-
-\subsection{From dense training to compensated sparse weights}
-Dense training saves unperturbed weights together with their matching geometric state. At pruning time, calibration activations determine the damped curvature, while the saved state resolves near-optimal support choices. Virtual conditioning is used only to select successive deletions; no virtual weight is committed. Each selected set is then compensated jointly from the unmodified current weights. Algorithm~\ref{alg:apbr} presents the dense and post-training stages as one continuous procedure.
-
-\begin{algorithm}[H]
-\caption{APBR-SAM with conditional-greedy selection and joint OBS compensation}
-\label{alg:apbr}
-\footnotesize
-\begin{algorithmic}[1]
-\Require data $\mathcal D$, weights $w$, radius $\rho$, feedback rate $\gamma$, groups $\mathcal G$, tolerance $\tau$
-\State Initialize $s\gets0$ and $A\gets I$
-\For{each dense-training mini-batch $B_t$}
-    \State $g_{1,t}\gets\nabla_w\mathcal L_{B_t}(w_t)$
-    \If{$\|A_t^2g_{1,t}\|_2>0$}
-        \State Compute $\epsilon_t$ by Eq.~\eqref{eq:epsilon} and $p_t$ by Eq.~\eqref{eq:feedback}
-        \State Form candidate $\widehat{s}_{t+1}\gets s_t+\gamma(\mathbf 1-dp_t)$
-    \Else
-        \State $\epsilon_t\gets0$ and $\widehat{s}_{t+1}\gets s_t$
-    \EndIf
-    \State $g_{2,t}\gets\nabla_w\mathcal L_{B_t}(w_t+\epsilon_t)$
-    \State Restore $w_t$ and apply the base-optimizer update using $g_{2,t}$
-    \State Commit $s_{t+1}\gets\widehat{s}_{t+1}$ after the successful parameter update
-\EndFor
-\State Save the matched dense weights $w$ and geometric state $s$
-\For{each eligible layer}
-    \State Build the damped activation curvature and its conditional inverse $R$
-    \For{each N:M group $G\in\mathcal G$}
-        \State $D\gets\varnothing$, $w^{\mathrm v}\gets w_G$, $R^{\mathrm v}\gets R_G$
-        \For{$r=1,\ldots,M-N$}
-            \State $c_k\gets(w_k^{\mathrm v})^2/(2R_{kk}^{\mathrm v})$; form $\mathcal C_\tau$ by Eq.~\eqref{eq:near-opt-window}
-            \State $k^*\gets\arg\min_{k\in\mathcal C_\tau}|w_k^{\mathrm v}|\exp(-s_k)$; $D\gets D\cup\{k^*\}$
-            \If{$r<M-N$} \State Virtually condition $w^{\mathrm v},R^{\mathrm v}$ using Eq.~\eqref{eq:conditional-update} \EndIf
-        \EndFor
-        \State Discard virtual states and apply Eq.~\eqref{eq:joint-obs} to the original current weights
-    \EndFor
-\EndFor
-\State \Return compensated sparse weights and the strict N:M mask
-\end{algorithmic}
-\end{algorithm}
-
-\section{Experiments}
-\paragraph{Setup.}
-We evaluate ResNet-18 on CIFAR-100 under the same 2:4 sparsity target. SAM and Wanda~\cite{wanda} are reported at their mask-only endpoints, SparseGPT~\cite{sparsegpt} at its native selection-and-OBS endpoint, and APBR-SAM after conditional-greedy selection and joint OBS compensation. The first three rows use three independent runs; the APBR-SAM result is the completed two-seed exploratory mean and is marked explicitly. Complete implementation details are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:reproduction}.
-
-\subsection{Main results}\label{sec:protocol}
-Table~\ref{tab:main} reports one frozen post-pruning endpoint per method. We do not label the columns as ``before'' and ``after compensation'' because conditional selection changes the support itself; that decomposition would incorrectly attribute a mask change to weight compensation.
-\begin{table}[H]\centering
-\caption{CIFAR-100 accuracy at each method's frozen post-pruning endpoint. Unless marked, entries are three-seed mean $\pm$ sample SD. $\dagger$ denotes a completed two-seed exploratory mean.}\label{tab:main}
